<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anak Tomb No. 3</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/357">357</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anak Tomb No. 3 is a painted tomb near Anak in South Hwanghae Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, dated by an ink inscription to 357 CE. Its murals show its owner, probably Dong Shou, a refugee official from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with his wife, attendants, a kitchen and a great procession of soldiers. It is one of the earliest and largest Goguryeo-period tombs and part of the UNESCO-listed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/complex-of-koguryo-tombs">Complex of Koguryo Tombs</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The tomb stands on a small hillock in Yongsun, Anak. It is built of stone in an asymmetrical plan of five rooms laid out along a long south-north axis: the entryway, a front chamber, two flanking ear-chambers at its sides, and the main burial chamber, which has narrow galleries on its eastern and northern sides. Each room has a lantern ceiling, a form found widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and West Asia.</p><p>Before 357 the native custom in the mid-Yalu region was to build graves of piled-up cairns, and this is the first tomb there with intricate stone chambers. The scholar Minku Kim argues that its construction points to a non-indigenous origin: indigenous Goguryeo tombs north of the Yalu face south-west, while this tomb and others in the more heavily Chinese-influenced region are aligned to the cardinal directions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Two portraits face each other: a man seated upright in red <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> with a white kwan over a black inner kwan, flanked by smaller men, and a woman turned slightly towards him. She wears the Chinese guiyi, a multi-lap swallow-tail garment, and a hairstyle identical to one of Northern Wei. A procession in the corridor holds 250 people, among them the owner in a cow-pulled wagon, a marching band, flag bearers, maids and civil officials. The eastern chamber shows a kitchen, a meat store, a barn, a carriage shed and household staff.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The Korean archaeologist Toh Yu-ho first analysed the tomb in 1946, when it had already been disturbed, though the murals and some inscriptions survived. The longest inscription, 68 characters in seven lines of regular script, stands above a door guardian on the wall leading to the western chamber. In 1951 the Chinese scholar Yeh Pai identified the Dong Shou it names with the man recorded in the Book of Jin and the Zizhi Tongjian as having fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> in 337, a reading accepted in the formal Korean report of 1958.</p><p>Some Korean scholars still hold that the tomb belonged to King Micheon or King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gogugwon-of-goguryeo">Gogugwon of Goguryeo</a>, and Hwi-joon Ahn and Youngsook Pak have argued that its size and paintings mark a royal tomb. Others regard Dong Shou as an émigré official serving Goguryeo. Minku Kim, noting that the epitaph lists his titles without any sign of servility and uses the Eastern Jin era name Yonghe, argues that he ruled the region as his own domain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anak Tomb No. 3” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
